<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Canice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 515–c. 600</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Cainnech of Aghaboe, Saint Kenneth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Canice, or Cainnech, was an Irish abbot and missionary of the 6th century, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>. He founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe</a> in Osraige and is said to have worked among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. He is the patron saint of the diocese of Ossory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>, whose name means 'church of Canice', grew around a church dedicated to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canice.jpg" alt="Saint Canice" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sheila1988 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_St_Cainneach_(Canice),_Kilkenny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Much of what is known of Cainnech comes from tradition and legend, though he is mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>, abbot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-columba">Life of Columba</a>. Cainnech moccu Dalánn was born at Glengiven, near Dungiven in Ulster. His father, Lughadh Leithdhearg, was a bard of the Corco Dalann who became tutor to the son of the chief of Cianachta, and his mother, Maul or Mella, was honoured for her holiness by a church beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>.</p><p>In 543 he became a pupil of Finnian at Clonard, one of the twelve students later known as the Twelve Apostles of Ireland, and there became a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>. He then studied under Mobhí Clárainech at Glasnevin, and when plague scattered that community he went to Cadoc's monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, where he was ordained priest in 545. After a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he returned to Glengiven in 550 and converted his foster brother, Geal Breagach, who helped him found Drumachose near Limavady.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 565 Cainnech joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where he is known as Kenneth. Adomnán tells that Columba foresaw his coming and that God gave him a calm crossing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> on a stormy day. He is said to have built a church at the place now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">Saint Andrews</a>, cells on the islands of Ibdon and Eninis, an oratory on Loch Laggan and a monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> on the Eden.</p><p>Back in Ireland he won the favour of Colmann son of Feradach, king of Osraige, who granted him land including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe</a>, the field of the ox, which became his chief monastery. Tradition holds that in 597 he led a Christian force against the last stronghold of druidic rule at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> and founded a monastery near the present St. Canice's Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In old age he retired to an island in Loch Cree and wrote a commentary on the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, known as the Glas-Chainnigh or Chain of Cainnech, which was long kept in his church. He was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe</a>. The see of Ossory stood there until the twelfth century, when it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>, and in 1346 Diarmaid Mac Giollaphádraig burned Aghaboe and destroyed his shrine and relics. His feast is kept on 11 October, and he is also a patron saint of the shipwrecked.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cainnech of Aghaboe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The monasteries of Saighir, linked with St Ciarán, and later Aghaboe and Kilkenny, where St <b>Canice</b> is honoured, were centres of learning and gave their names to the diocese of Ossory.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-osraige">Kingdom of Osraige</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Drumramer, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.05,-6.83&amp;year=570" aria-label="Drumramer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4400 282 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saint Canice"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 552h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 552h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.05,-6.83&amp;year=570">What was here in 570 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
